window.MAPLE_SEARCH=[{"p":"Licence","h":"Licence","u":"license.html","x":"Licence Copyright (c) 2026 Kinn Coelho Juliao <kinncj@gmail.com>. All rights reserved. MAPLE HARNESS — BINARY LICENCE (FREE FOR NON-COMMERCIAL USE) 1. Non-commercial use of the binaries. Permission is granted, free of charge, to any person to download, install, run and redistribute unmodified copies of the compiled Map"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"Measure with bench","u":"bench.html","x":"`maple-harness bench` answers one question on your own work: does a setting change what a task costs, and does the result stay as good? It replays the same tasks under different settings (\"arms\"), several times each, in fresh directories, and compares the arms task by task with the uncertainty shown. It is an advanced "},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"Try it without spending","u":"bench.html#try-it-without-spending","x":"With no `--yes` the command prints the plan (how many runs, in what arms) and an estimate of the cost from earlier runs of the same suite, or says the cost is unknown. Nothing is run and nothing is spent."},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"Run it for real","u":"bench.html#run-it-for-real","x":"`--k 3` runs every task three times under every arm. Three is the least that says anything about variation. `--budget-ai-units` (or `--budget-tokens`) is required with `--yes`. The bench stops before the run that would pass it, judged by the average cost so far. One run that costs far more than the average can still pa"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"Write your own tasks","u":"bench.html#write-your-own-tasks","x":"A suite is a folder: `task.yaml`: Instead of `files/` a task can name a repository and a commit to check out (`repo:` and `commit:`). Source files that a build would otherwise pick up as part of its own project can carry a `.tmpl` suffix, which is removed on copy. A task without `verify` has no check, and its quality i"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"Compare another tool on the same model","u":"bench.html#compare-another-tool-on-the-same-model","x":"An arm can be another program instead of a change to this one. It gets the same tasks, in the same fresh directories, with the same check, so the comparison is like for like. The example below runs GitHub Copilot CLI against a model you host yourself, next to Maple Harness on the same model. No shell. The command is a "},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"Counting on the model server","u":"bench.html#counting-on-the-model-server","x":"Measured in AI units or the tool's own tokens, two programs cannot be compared, because one may report nothing. If your model server is vLLM, point the bench at its metrics and it reads the server's own counters around every run: Before each run it checks that the server is idle (nothing running or waiting). If it is n"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"A check for a person, too","u":"bench.html#a-check-for-a-person-too","x":"Tests can pass on something that does not work. A task may add `verify_browser: check.py` (a script in the task folder; `.js` runs with node, `.py` with python, `.sh` with sh). It runs after the tests, gets the working directory and the machine's numeric network address in `MAPLE_BENCH_LAN_IP`, and is typically a Playw"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"Read the results","u":"bench.html#read-the-results","x":"The report shows, for each arm, the median with a 95% interval; then each arm against the baseline, task by task: the change (negative means it used less), its interval, a p-value, and how many tasks it won, lost and tied; then the pass rate, so that a cheaper arm that fails more is visible; then the total each arm cos"},{"p":"Measure with bench","h":"What is recorded","u":"bench.html#what-is-recorded","x":"Each run records tokens, cache reads and writes, AI units, requests, retries, tool calls, wall time, files changed, and whether the check passed. No prompt text, file contents or credentials are recorded, and paths are removed from error messages unless you pass `--anonymize=false`. Related: how to read the cost of a s"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Commands and keys","u":"commands.html","x":"Everything you can type in a session, the keys, the modes, and the commands you run from a shell. For the settings in `config.yaml`, see Harness settings ; for the proxy, see Proxy settings . Each item has a Helps tag, because each exists to serve one or more of three goals: Efficient : fewer tokens or AI units for the"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Modes and keys","u":"commands.html#modes-and-keys","x":"Permission modes decide what happens when the model wants to write a file or run a command. Mode What happens Helps `ask` (default) You are asked before each write or command. `deny` rules are absolute Efficacious `auto` Writes and commands run without asking; `deny` rules still apply. Unlike `yolo`, it can be chosen w"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Slash commands","u":"commands.html#slash-commands","x":""},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Session","u":"commands.html#session","x":"Command What it does Helps `/help` Shows the commands and keys `/clear` Starts over: clears the screen and the conversation. Memory notes are kept Efficient `/quit`, `/exit` Exit `/sessions` Lists saved sessions. Resume one with `--session <name>` Effective `/fork [n]` Copies the first `n` messages of this conversation"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Models and effort","u":"commands.html#models-and-effort","x":"Command What it does Helps `/model` Opens a list of the models the proxy serves. Bare `/model` also shows what automatic choice would use for each kind of work Effective `/model <name>` Switches straight to that model or alias Effective `/model auto` Chooses a model per message from what your provider serves, within yo"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Context and cost","u":"commands.html#context-and-cost","x":"Command What it does Helps `/context` What the model can see: memory notes, the project index, the tools it is offered, the window used, and what the session has spent Efficient `/cost` What the session has spent: totals, the last requests with their cache hit rate (and where a model change or a low hit rate lost the c"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Memory, project and tools","u":"commands.html#memory-project-and-tools","x":"Command What it does Helps `/memory [list|show <name>|rm <name>|path]` The agent's notes about the project and you Effective `/graph [stats|reindex|connections <id>|path]` Queries and rebuilds the project index of notes and code symbols the harness looks things up in Efficient `/jobs` Lists background commands started "},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Safety","u":"commands.html#safety","x":"Command What it does Helps `/sandbox` How the `bash` tool is contained: the mode, the backend, what is writable and hidden. See The shell sandbox Efficacious `/sandbox auto`, `/sandbox strict`, `/sandbox off` Contains commands when a backend is available; refuses to run them without one; or no containment for this sess"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Agents","u":"commands.html#agents","x":"Command What it does Helps `/agents` Manage subagents: list, view, create, edit, delete Effective `/agent <name> <task>`, `@name <task>` Runs a subagent on a task Efficient, Effective"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Remote and notifications","u":"commands.html#remote-and-notifications","x":"Command What it does Helps `/remote` Shares this session with a phone or another Maple Harness. See Control it from your phone Effective `/remote pair` Opens a list of addresses, then makes a short pairing code and one QR for the address you pick. `--via`, `--token` and `--fixed` change what it makes; `--watch-only` ma"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Agents and the agents tray","u":"commands.html#agents-and-the-agents-tray","x":"Subagents are helper agents you define in `.claude/agents/<name>.md` (or `~/.claude/agents`), or use from the built-in set. The model can hand them jobs, and you can run one yourself with `@name <task>` (`Tab` completes the name). While any run, a tray sits just above the message box: a `main` row and one row per subag"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Your own commands and skills","u":"commands.html#your-own-commands-and-skills","x":"`.claude/commands/<name>.md` becomes `/name` (with `$ARGUMENTS`, or `$1` to `$9`, substituted). `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` becomes a skill, callable with `/name` or chosen by the model. Built-in commands always win over a command or skill with the same name. Effective : your team's routines are one keystroke away"},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Commands you run from a shell","u":"commands.html#commands-you-run-from-a-shell","x":"`maple-harness` with no command starts the full-screen interface. The short name is `mpl`. On a terminal, commands that print lists or status use tables and your theme's colours, fitted to the window. Piped into another program or a file, the output is plain text with no colour codes and no cut-off cells. `--no-color` "},{"p":"Commands and keys","h":"Command-line flags","u":"commands.html#command-line-flags","x":"Flag What it does `--prompt \"text\"` Runs one turn and exits, printing the answer. Everything else goes to the error stream, so `> out.txt` captures just the answer `--json-summary FILE` With `--prompt`: writes a machine-readable summary of the run (tokens, cache figures, AI units, tool calls, why it stopped) `--continu"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Harness settings","u":"configuration.html","x":"The harness reads the settings under `harness:` in `config.yaml` (usually `~/.config/maple-harness/config.yaml`). The proxy's settings are in the same file and are covered in the Proxy settings . Changes under `harness:` apply the next time you start the harness; the proxy picks up its own changes without a restart. Ev"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Settings at a glance","u":"configuration.html#settings-at-a-glance","x":"Key Default Helps `default_model` none Effective `compress_with_model` `off` Efficient `compress_model`, `compress_scope` none (chosen for you), `same-account` Efficient, Efficacious `digest_key_points` `on` Effective `auto_model`, `auto_model_map`, `auto_avoid`, `auto_model_resolution` off, none, `*-codex*`, `client` "},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Per-project overrides","u":"configuration.html#per-project-overrides","x":"A project can carry its own `.mapleharness/config.yaml` (or `.mapleharness/config.json`) that changes some of `harness:`'s settings just for that project, on top of your global `config.yaml`. It only needs the keys it changes: Anything the project's file does not mention keeps your global value — setting `loop_guard.mo"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Model choice","u":"configuration.html#model-choice","x":"Key Default What it does Trade-off Helps `default_model` none The model or alias a session starts on. With none set, it is `gpt-6-sol` when the proxy serves it, else the first model that can use tools. If you name one the proxy does not serve, the harness starts on the first model it does serve that can use tools, and "},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Long conversations","u":"configuration.html#long-conversations","x":"When a conversation outgrows the model's window, older messages leave the model's view. Nothing is thrown away: Older parts of a long conversation stay searchable. Every message is stored, word for word, beside the saved session before it leaves the model's view, and the model can look any of it up again with the `hist"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Permissions and safety","u":"configuration.html#permissions-and-safety","x":"Key Default What it does Trade-off Helps `permission.mode` `ask` The starting mode: `ask`, `auto` or `yolo`. `yolo` is honoured only when you start with `--yolo` Efficacious `permission.rules[].pattern`, `permission.rules[].decision` shipped rules Rules matched in order: `decision` is `allow`, `ask` or `deny`, and `den"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Runaways and long output","u":"configuration.html#runaways-and-long-output","x":"Key Default What it does Trade-off Helps `loop_guard.mode` `stop` What to do when the same problem keeps failing after different attempts: `off`, `warn` (one note to the model), or `stop` (the note, then the turn ends with a summary for you) `stop` may end a run you would have let continue; `warn` never ends one Effici"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Language servers and shell writes","u":"configuration.html#language-servers-and-shell-writes","x":"Key Default What it does Trade-off Helps `lsp` `auto` With no `lsp_servers`, finds language servers from the project's marker files and `PATH`, and adds their errors to the result of each file write or edit, so the model fixes its own mistakes. `off` turns detection, the diagnostics and the lookup tools off The first e"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Tools, skills and hooks","u":"configuration.html#tools-skills-and-hooks","x":"Key Default What it does Helps `mcp_servers[].name` required The server's name, used in permission rules such as `mcp:name(*)` Effective `mcp_servers[].type` inferred `http` or `sse` for a remote server. A command means a local one Effective `mcp_servers[].command`, `mcp_servers[].args`, `mcp_servers[].env` none A loca"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Subagents and remote control","u":"configuration.html#subagents-and-remote-control","x":"Key Default What it does Trade-off Helps `subagents.max_parallel` `3` How many subagents run at once (1 to 16). More wait for a free slot A local model may not serve several at once Efficient, Effective `subagents.max_turns` `50` The most turns any one subagent may take, whatever its own limit asks for (1 to 200) Effic"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Notifications","u":"configuration.html#notifications","x":"Messages to your phone or desktop when a session needs approval, stops with an error, or needs your guidance. They say which project and what happened, and nothing else: never a command, a file's content or an error's text. Off until you list a destination. Try it with `maple-harness notify test`. Separately from these"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Housekeeping and appearance","u":"configuration.html#housekeeping-and-appearance","x":"Key Default What it does Helps `retention.sessions_days` `30` How old a saved session must be before `cleanup` treats it as old Efficient `retention.keep_per_dir` `5` How many recent sessions per directory `cleanup` always keeps Efficient `retention.auto_prune` off Run what `cleanup --yes` would, once a day, when a ses"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"Where the harness finds the proxy","u":"configuration.html#where-the-harness-finds-the-proxy","x":"Key Default What it does `version` `1` The configuration file's format version `server.openai_compat` `127.0.0.1:11535` The proxy's OpenAI-compatible address, which the harness talks to `server.anthropic_compat` `127.0.0.1:11537` The proxy's Anthropic-compatible address `server.webui` `127.0.0.1:11539` The proxy's setu"},{"p":"Harness settings","h":"An annotated example","u":"configuration.html#an-annotated-example","x":"Read next: Commands and keys , Proxy settings , and Settings by goal ."},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"What /cost tells you","u":"cost.html","x":"`/cost` is the session's own bill, read back to you in plain numbers: what has been spent, request by request, and which parts of the conversation are costing the most by being sent again and again. This page walks through each part of its output and, for each thing it can flag, what to actually do about it. It makes n"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"The totals line","u":"cost.html#the-totals-line","x":"The first line is the session so far: input and output tokens, how many requests were billed, and — where the provider states one — AI units (Copilot) or an estimated dollar figure (see Anthropic and estimated cost below). Under it: the model in use, how many requests were billed against how many were asked again after"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"Subagents","u":"cost.html#subagents","x":"If this session delegated any work with `@agent` or the `delegate` tool, `/cost` ends with a \"Subagents\" line — how many ran, and what they cost on their own — and a \"Session + subagents\" line, which is the true total: the main conversation's own spend plus every subagent's. This is the number that matches what your ac"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"The last requests table","u":"cost.html#the-last-requests-table","x":"A row per recent request: input, cached, written and output tokens, AI units, the cache hit rate for that one request, and the model. Two things get flagged automatically: \"model changed\" — a different model answered this request than the one before it. A new model starts with nothing cached, so the whole conversation "},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"Where the next request's input goes","u":"cost.html#where-the-next-requests-input-goes","x":"An estimated breakdown of what the next request would send, by kind: the system prompt, the tools on offer, your own messages, anything looked up on your behalf, the model's own replies and tool calls, and tool results. It is a token-count estimate (about four characters to a token), useful for proportions rather than "},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"Most expensive tool results","u":"cost.html#most-expensive-tool-results","x":"A tool's result is not paid for once. It stays in the conversation and is sent again on every request after it, until it is trimmed or the session ends — so a single large result from early in a long session can end up costing far more than its own size ever suggested. This section ranks the tool results still in the c"},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"Anthropic and estimated cost","u":"cost.html#anthropic-and-estimated-cost","x":"Copilot states a price for every call it serves. Claude through Anthropic's own API does not — its responses carry token counts only, never a price. `/cost` reflects that honestly: an Anthropic request shows its tokens like any other, and no price, unless the provider is set up to estimate one. An operator can turn on "},{"p":"What /cost tells you","h":"Related","u":"cost.html#related","x":"See and reduce what a task costs · Proxy settings · Commands and keys · Settings by goal"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"Connect a model server","u":"endpoints.html","x":"Any server that speaks the OpenAI API can be a provider: Ollama, LM Studio, oMLX, Lemonade, Unsloth Studio, vLLM, llama.cpp, an MLX server, or another. You can add one from the setup page or by editing the configuration file."},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"From the setup page","u":"endpoints.html#from-the-setup-page","x":"Open `http://127.0.0.1:11539` and choose Add provider . Pick the type. The usual address for that server is filled in. Press Detect models to list what the server has loaded, then save. It applies immediately. No restart is needed."},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"By hand","u":"endpoints.html#by-hand","x":"Add a provider to `~/.config/maple-harness/config.yaml`: Field Meaning `id` A name for this provider `type` What kind of server it is (table below) `base_url` The server's address including `/v1` (Lemonade uses `/api/v1`) `optional: true` If the server is not running, skip it instead of failing to start. Good for anyth"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"Types and their usual addresses","u":"endpoints.html#types-and-their-usual-addresses","x":"`type` Server Address `ollama` Ollama `http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1` `lmstudio` LM Studio `http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1` `omlx` oMLX (Apple silicon) `http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1` `lemonade` Lemonade Server (AMD) `http://127.0.0.1:13305/api/v1` `unsloth` Unsloth Studio (expects an API key) `http://127.0.0.1:8888/v1` `openai-compa"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"API keys","u":"endpoints.html#api-keys","x":"If a server needs a key, do not paste it into the file. Refer to it: A key you type on the setup page is stored in the keyring for you."},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"Use your models from other tools","u":"endpoints.html#use-your-models-from-other-tools","x":"Any tool that speaks the OpenAI or Anthropic API can use the proxy. For what a tool gains from it (every model the proxy serves, fallbacks, budgets, the usage log and prompt caching) and what stays in Maple Harness, see What the proxy adds for other tools . Point it at the proxy's addresses: API Address Path OpenAI-com"},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"Claude Code","u":"endpoints.html#claude-code","x":"Claude Code speaks the Anthropic API. Set these in your shell, or in the `env` block of `~/.claude/settings.json`: Without `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_HAIKU_MODEL`, Claude Code asks the proxy for its own small model by name for background work, which fails unless the proxy serves that name."},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"OpenCode","u":"endpoints.html#opencode","x":"Add a provider to `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json`: Each key under `models` is a name the proxy lists. Pick one with `/models`, or `opencode run -m maple/local \"...\"`."},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"pi","u":"endpoints.html#pi","x":"Add a provider to `~/.pi/agent/models.json`: pi needs the `apiKey` to show the models; any value works on this machine. Pick a model with `/model`, or `pi --model maple/local`."},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"GitHub Copilot CLI","u":"endpoints.html#github-copilot-cli","x":"Copilot CLI's bring-your-own-model mode is set with environment variables, and needs no GitHub sign-in: Leave `COPILOT_PROVIDER_TYPE` and `COPILOT_PROVIDER_WIRE_API` at their defaults (`openai` and `completions`). For a proxy on another machine, put the access token in `COPILOT_PROVIDER_API_KEY`."},{"p":"Connect a model server","h":"Check it","u":"endpoints.html#check-it","x":"If Detect models finds nothing, the server is usually not running or the address is missing its `/v1`. See Troubleshooting ."},{"p":"Your first session","h":"Your first session","u":"getting-started.html","x":"A first session in three steps."},{"p":"Your first session","h":"1. Start the proxy","u":"getting-started.html#1-start-the-proxy","x":"The proxy is the one program that talks to models and keeps your sign-ins. The agent starts it for you if nothing is listening, so you can skip this step, but running it yourself lets you watch what it does. On first run it looks for Ollama and Apple's on-device model and writes a working starter configuration. It open"},{"p":"Your first session","h":"2. Sign in or add a model","u":"getting-started.html#2-sign-in-or-add-a-model","x":"Open `http://127.0.0.1:11539` and either sign in to GitHub Copilot, save an Anthropic API key, or add a local model server. Or do it from the terminal: Details for each are in Sign in and Connect a model server ."},{"p":"Your first session","h":"3. Start the agent","u":"getting-started.html#3-start-the-agent","x":"Go to a project directory and run: The start screen in a project called acme-api. Type in the box at the bottom; `/help` lists every command. Type what you want done. Useful first commands: Command What it does `/model` Choose a model from the list, or `/model auto` to let the agent choose per message `/help` List ever"},{"p":"Your first session","h":"Where things are kept","u":"getting-started.html#where-things-are-kept","x":"Everything lives in `~/.config/maple-harness/` (`%APPDATA%\\maple-harness` on Windows): the configuration file `config.yaml`, saved sessions, memory notes, and your sign-ins. Sign-ins are kept in your system keyring where there is one, not in the configuration file."},{"p":"Your first session","h":"Next","u":"getting-started.html#next","x":"Every command, key and setting, with what it does and its trade-off: Commands and keys , Harness settings and the Proxy settings . For the setting that fits a goal, see Settings by goal ."},{"p":"Glossary","h":"Glossary","u":"glossary.html","x":"The words these pages use, in alphabetical order, each with the page that covers it. Access token. A secret that lets a device or another machine use what runs on this one: a paired phone driving or watching sessions, or another machine using the proxy's model APIs. Each token carries only what it is for, and you can r"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"Use it with herdr","u":"herdr.html","x":"herdr hosts terminal sessions for coding agents and shows, for each pane, whether the agent in it is working, waiting for you, or idle. Maple Harness works with it without any setup."},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"What you need to do","u":"herdr.html#what-you-need-to-do","x":"Nothing. Start `maple-harness` (or `mpl`) inside a herdr pane and the pane labels itself `maple-harness` and shows its state as it changes: State What it means working a turn is running blocked it is waiting for you to approve a tool call idle it is waiting for your next message The harness tells herdr itself, over her"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"If the pane does not pick it up","u":"herdr.html#if-the-pane-does-not-pick-it-up","x":"Check that you started the harness from a shell inside the herdr pane (not a separate terminal window). If herdr uses a non-default socket, make sure `HERDR_SOCKET_PATH` points at it in the environment the harness inherits. By default it looks in `~/.config/herdr/herdr.sock`. Run `maple-harness version` to confirm you "},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"The terminal signals, with or without herdr","u":"herdr.html#the-terminal-signals-with-or-without-herdr","x":"Independently of herdr, the harness sets the window title and the terminal's progress indicator on every change of state, and sends a notification when a turn finishes or needs a decision. Terminals that do not support one of these ignore it. State Window title Progress indicator idle `● MapleHarness` cleared working `"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"The detection manifest (advanced)","u":"herdr.html#the-detection-manifest-advanced","x":"herdr can also classify a pane from a detection manifest. Most people do not need this, because the harness reports its own state. If you want the manifest anyway, `maple-harness herdr-manifest` prints it, and `maple-harness herdr-manifest --id <name>` writes a different agent id into it. Whether your herdr version loa"},{"p":"Use it with herdr","h":"Turning the installer's herdr step off","u":"herdr.html#turning-the-installers-herdr-step-off","x":"The installer needs to install nothing for herdr. `--no-herdr` is accepted and ignored, so older install commands keep working. Related: terminal notifications and their settings are in Harness settings , and the `herdr-manifest` command is in Commands and keys ."},{"p":"Overview","h":"Overview","u":"index.html","x":"Maple Harness is a coding agent for GitHub Copilot's models, Claude and models on your own machine. It comes in three parts: The agent , `maple-harness`: the program you work with in a terminal. It reads your project, edits files and runs commands, and asks before it changes anything. The proxy , `maple-proxy`: holds y"},{"p":"Overview","h":"Start here","u":"index.html#start-here","x":"Install the programs. Run Your first session . Sign in to GitHub Copilot or Claude, or Connect a model server such as Ollama or LM Studio. Choose a model , or let the agent choose for each message."},{"p":"Overview","h":"Get started","u":"index.html#get-started","x":"Installing, signing in and connecting models. Install : the one-line installer, platforms, updating and removing it. Your first session : start the proxy and the agent, and the first commands to know. Sign in : GitHub Copilot, an Anthropic API key, and more than one account. Connect a model server : Ollama, LM Studio, "},{"p":"Overview","h":"Guides","u":"index.html#guides","x":"How each part works, and how to use it with other tools. The agent Subagents : hand small jobs to helper agents, or make your own. Language servers : compile errors right after every edit. Search by meaning : let the agent find things by what they mean, not only the words. The shell sandbox : contain the commands the m"},{"p":"Overview","h":"Cost and usage","u":"index.html#cost-and-usage","x":"Where the spend goes, how to keep it down, and how to check any claim on your own tasks. See and reduce what a task costs : start here. What /cost tells you : each part of `/cost`, and what to do about it. Settings by goal : the setting for spending less, keeping quality or stopping runaways. Your usage data : query an"},{"p":"Overview","h":"Reference","u":"index.html#reference","x":"Every command, key and setting, with what it does, its default and its trade-off. Commands and keys : slash commands, keys, modes and shell commands. Harness settings : every `harness:` setting in `config.yaml`. Proxy settings : every proxy setting, setup-page card, command and response header."},{"p":"Overview","h":"Help","u":"index.html#help","x":"When something does not work, and the rest. Troubleshooting : common problems and their fixes. Security : what it can touch and how to keep it safe. Glossary : the words these pages use. Licence . Release notes , with the downloads."},{"p":"Install","h":"Install","u":"install.html","x":"On every system Maple Harness is two programs: `maple-harness`, the agent, and `maple-proxy`, the proxy, which holds your sign-ins and talks to the models. On macOS and Linux the installer also adds the short names `mpl` and `mplp`. On a Mac with Apple silicon there is a third program, `maple-sidecar`, a small helper f"},{"p":"Install","h":"One line","u":"install.html#one-line","x":"macOS and Linux: Windows (PowerShell): No account or token is needed. The installer detects your system, downloads the release, checks every file against the release's `SHA256SUMS`, and refuses to install anything that does not match. To read it first, open it on GitHub . On macOS and Linux it installs to `/usr/local/b"},{"p":"Install","h":"Platforms","u":"install.html#platforms","x":"System Agent and proxy Apple on-device model macOS, Apple silicon yes yes (macOS 26 or newer) macOS, Intel yes no Linux, amd64 and arm64 yes no Windows, amd64 yes (less tested) no macOS. The shell sandbox is built in: nothing to install. The on-device model needs a Mac with Apple silicon and macOS 26 or newer; the agen"},{"p":"Install","h":"Optional: contain the shell (bubblewrap)","u":"install.html#optional-contain-the-shell-bubblewrap","x":"The agent can run the commands its shell tool executes inside a sandbox. On macOS that is built in. On Linux it needs bubblewrap, which is optional : without it the shell still works, uncontained. System Command Arch, Manjaro `sudo pacman -S bubblewrap` Debian, Ubuntu `sudo apt install bubblewrap` Fedora, RHEL `sudo dn"},{"p":"Install","h":"Release files","u":"install.html#release-files","x":"Each release on the releases page has these files. The installer picks the right ones; you only need this list to download by hand. File What it is `maple-harness_linux_amd64`, `maple-harness_linux_arm64`, `maple-harness_darwin_arm64`, `maple-harness_darwin_amd64`, `maple-harness_windows_amd64.exe` The agent `maple-pro"},{"p":"Install","h":"Update","u":"install.html#update","x":""},{"p":"Install","h":"Uninstall","u":"install.html#uninstall","x":"The second line deletes your configuration, stored sign-ins, memory notes and saved sessions. Skip it if you plan to reinstall."},{"p":"Install","h":"Next","u":"install.html#next","x":"Your first session ."},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Language servers","u":"language-servers.html","x":"Language servers give the agent compile errors right after every edit. A language server is the program your editor uses to underline errors, jump to a definition and show a type on hover, and Maple Harness can use the same programs. With one installed for your project's language: Errors are shown right after each edit"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"How it finds servers","u":"language-servers.html#how-it-finds-servers","x":"By default (`harness.lsp: auto`), the agent looks at the folder you started it in. If it finds a file that marks a language, and the matching server is on your `PATH`, that server is used for that language. Nothing else is needed. If you name servers yourself in `harness.lsp_servers`, those are used exactly as you wrot"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Check what it found","u":"language-servers.html#check-what-it-found","x":"Type `/lsp` in a session. It lists each server as running , idle (installed, starts when first needed) or missing , and prints the install command for a missing one. `maple-harness doctor` has a \"Language servers\" line too. A missing server is only a warning: everything still works, the agent just doesn't get compile e"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Install a server","u":"language-servers.html#install-a-server","x":"Start the agent in the project's root folder, the one with the marker file. The first two columns say what triggers a language and which server is used. Language Marker files in the project folder Server Install Go `go.mod`, `go.work` `gopls` `go install golang.org/x/tools/gopls@latest` TypeScript, JavaScript `tsconfig"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Gherkin and Cucumber","u":"language-servers.html#gherkin-and-cucumber","x":"If your project describes behaviour in `.feature` files (behaviour-driven development), the Cucumber language server checks them. The most useful thing it does is report a step with no matching step definition , so when the agent writes a scenario, it learns immediately which steps still need code. A typical project: W"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Choose the servers yourself","u":"language-servers.html#choose-the-servers-yourself","x":"Add `lsp_servers` to `config.yaml`. Each entry names the file extensions, the command and its arguments. This replaces detection."},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Turn it off","u":"language-servers.html#turn-it-off","x":"With `lsp: off` no server is started, no errors are added to results and the three lookup tools are not offered. `lsp: auto` is the default."},{"p":"Language servers","h":"When the agent still writes files with echo and cat","u":"language-servers.html#when-the-agent-still-writes-files-with-echo-and-cat","x":"The agent is told to create and change files with its own file tools, because those show you a diff, apply the safety checks and run the language server. Some models still write files through the shell, for example with `cat > file <<EOF`. By default the agent notes it in that command's result so the model can correct "},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"language-servers.html#troubleshooting","x":"Installed, but `/lsp` says missing. The agent looks on the `PATH` it was started with. A tool installed by `npm i -g` or `go install` lives in a folder that must be on that `PATH` (for Go, `~/go/bin`). Start the agent from a shell where the command works, and check with `which gopls` (or the server's name). No server i"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"What has been tried","u":"language-servers.html#what-has-been-tried","x":"Stated plainly, so you know what to expect: The way the agent starts servers, sends them each edit and reads back their errors, and the answers it gives to the requests servers make of an editor, has been tested against a stand-in server in the test suite. The Cucumber language server was run for real: it reported \"Und"},{"p":"Language servers","h":"Related","u":"language-servers.html#related","x":"The settings are in Harness settings , and `/lsp` is in Commands and keys . Other problems: Troubleshooting ."},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","u":"mcp.html","x":"`maple-harness mcp serve` lets any tool that speaks MCP (Claude Code, OpenCode, another Maple Harness) read this project's notes and code map, the same ones the agent uses. Build the index once from the project directory: Then connect a tool. A client normally launches the server itself."},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"Claude Code","u":"mcp.html#claude-code","x":"or in the project's `.mcp.json`: Add `\"--read-only\"` to the arguments to withhold the tools that write notes."},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"OpenCode","u":"mcp.html#opencode","x":"In `opencode.json`:"},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"Another Maple Harness","u":"mcp.html#another-maple-harness","x":"In `config.yaml`:"},{"p":"Give other tools this project's knowledge","h":"Using MCP servers from the agent","u":"mcp.html#using-mcp-servers-from-the-agent","x":"The agent can use MCP servers too, from the `harness.mcp_servers` list or a project's `.mcp.json`. Their tools ask for approval like any other tool. A server that runs as a program on your machine runs with your permissions, so add only servers you trust. A server reached over the network needs `https` unless it is on "},{"p":"Choose a model","h":"Choose a model","u":"models.html","x":"Switch models at any time inside the agent: Which models appear depends on what you have configured: the ones your Copilot plan allows, the ones your Anthropic key can call, and whatever your local servers have loaded. The agent also shows how much of the model's context is used, in the status bar. `/context` shows the"},{"p":"Choose a model","h":"Automatic choice","u":"models.html#automatic-choice","x":"`/model auto` chooses a model for each message from your current provider's own ranking of its models, and tells you what it chose and why. For example, the status bar reads `gpt-4.1 (auto: heavy)` and the first message prints a line such as `model: auto → gpt-4.1 (heavy; no powerful model on this account; used versati"},{"p":"Choose a model","h":"A default model","u":"models.html#a-default-model","x":"Set the model the agent starts with in `config.yaml`: If the configured model is not one your proxy serves, the agent starts on the first usable model it does serve and prints a one-line note listing the real names."},{"p":"Choose a model","h":"Effort and thinking","u":"models.html#effort-and-thinking","x":"Related: every model setting is in Harness settings ; to fall back to another model or account when one cannot answer, see Fallbacks ; for what a model costs you, see See and reduce what a task costs ."},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"What the proxy adds for other tools","u":"other-tools.html","x":"Claude Code, OpenCode, pi and GitHub Copilot CLI can send their requests through the Maple proxy instead of straight to a provider. Use your models from other tools has a tested setup for each. This page says what they get from it, and what stays in Maple Harness. Other tools that speak the OpenAI or Anthropic API, suc"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"At a glance","u":"other-tools.html#at-a-glance","x":"What each tool sent was checked with Claude Code 2.1.284, OpenCode 1.18.33, pi 0.87.1 and Copilot CLI 1.0.89. Other versions may send different headers or cache marks. Claude Code OpenCode pi Copilot CLI Every model the proxy serves Yes Yes Yes Yes Aliases, fallbacks, budgets Once you set them Once you set them Once yo"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"One address for many models","u":"other-tools.html#one-address-for-many-models","x":"The tool asks for a model by name, and the proxy sends the request to whichever provider serves it: your GitHub Copilot account, an Anthropic account, or a model server such as Ollama, LM Studio or oMLX. The proxy translates between the two APIs in both directions. Claude Code, which speaks the Anthropic API, can use a"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"Keeps going when an account runs out","u":"other-tools.html#keeps-going-when-an-account-runs-out","x":"Fallbacks apply to every request through the proxy, whatever tool sent it. With `model_fallbacks`, a model that cannot answer (out of chats or credit, overloaded, unreachable) hands the request to the next name on its list. `provider_fallbacks` moves one account's requests to another account. A model that has just said"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"Budgets","u":"other-tools.html#budgets","x":"A daily limit on a provider (requests, AI units, or both) counts every request to that provider through the proxy, whichever tool sent it. At the limit, requests are refused or handed to a fallback. See Daily budgets ."},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"See what each tool spends","u":"other-tools.html#see-what-each-tool-spends","x":"Every request through the proxy gets one line in the usage log: the model asked for and the model that answered, tokens in and out, tokens read from and written to the cache, AI units when the provider reports them, and estimated cost and savings columns. The log keeps counts and labels, never prompts or answers. You c"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"Prompt cache","u":"other-tools.html#prompt-cache","x":"Providers charge less for the start of a request they have already seen. For Claude models, reached through an Anthropic account or through Copilot, the provider caches only the parts of a request that are marked for caching. The proxy marks them (`prompt_cache`, on by default): Claude Code marks its own system prompt."},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"Settings you can turn on","u":"other-tools.html#settings-you-can-turn-on","x":"These are off by default. When on, they apply to every tool. Image shrinking (`image_resize_tool`, `image_resize_user`, `image_superseded`) sends smaller images. Small text in a screenshot may become harder to read. Snapshot trimming (`snapshot_trim`) shortens browser page snapshots from Playwright MCP. It only acts on"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"Using it from another machine","u":"other-tools.html#using-it-from-another-machine","x":"To reach the proxy from another machine, give the tool an access token as its API key, in place of the placeholder. The token's label then names the tool in the usage log's `client` column. The setup page itself is reachable only from the proxy's own machine, and you can protect it with a password . See Using it from a"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"What stays in Maple Harness","u":"other-tools.html#what-stays-in-maple-harness","x":"These belong to the agent, not the proxy. Pointing another tool at the proxy does not give it any of them: `history_search` over earlier conversations, and the session journal of what each turn read, changed and ran. Memory notes and the project index as the agent uses them: recalled during a turn, and kept current aft"},{"p":"What the proxy adds for other tools","h":"Measure it yourself","u":"other-tools.html#measure-it-yourself","x":"The measured comparison on this website is between Maple Harness and Copilot CLI. What the proxy changes for these tools on their own has not been measured, so this page gives no figure. To measure it on your own work, use \u00000\u0000 . It can run another tool as one arm, with the same tasks and the same check, several times e"},{"p":"Check it yourself","h":"Check it yourself","u":"proof.html","x":"Maple Harness is built to make a task cost fewer tokens without making the work worse. This page says what that means, how to check it on your own tasks, and what we hold ourselves to. It makes no promise about your bill: that depends on your tasks and your models."},{"p":"Check it yourself","h":"What we aim for","u":"proof.html#what-we-aim-for","x":"Efficient. Maple Harness is built to spend fewer tokens for the same work: it keeps requests in a form your provider can reuse, keeps the conversation lean, and stops waste before it compounds. Efficacious. It does not trade quality for tokens: it does not ask the model to talk in shorthand, what the model sees is cut "},{"p":"Check it yourself","h":"Don't take our word for it","u":"proof.html#dont-take-our-word-for-it","x":"Please be diligent. A saving that only shows up in one request, or one run, is not a saving. Here is how to check. Compare whole runs, three against three. Pick a task you run often. Run it three times with a setting on and three times with it off, for example trimming of old tool output, or letting the agent choose th"},{"p":"Check it yourself","h":"What we hold ourselves to","u":"proof.html#what-we-hold-ourselves-to","x":"We publish results only from repeated, paired runs on real tasks, including the runs where a setting did not help, with the uncertainty shown. A technique is on by default only if quality is not worse. Nothing here is a promise about your bill."},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Keep it running in the background","u":"proxy-service.html","x":"By default the agent starts the proxy for you when you open it, and it stops when you are done. Run the proxy as a service when you want it to be already there: before you open a terminal, for other tools that use your models, or for your phone. `maple-harness service install` does it in one command. It writes one smal"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"In short","u":"proxy-service.html#in-short","x":"`maple-harness service` keeps the proxy and the phone gateway running in the background and starts them when you log in. On Linux it writes systemd user units; on macOS, launchd agents . You do not need this to use the agent: it starts the proxy itself, and `/remote` starts the gateway. Install a service when you want "},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"What you get","u":"proxy-service.html#what-you-get","x":"Linux macOS The file it writes `~/.config/systemd/user/maple-proxy.service` `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.kinncj.maple-proxy.plist` Starts when you log in (at boot too, after one extra command below) when you log in If it stops with an error restarted 3 seconds later restarted 5 seconds later Logs `journalctl --user -u m"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Debian and Ubuntu","u":"proxy-service.html#debian-and-ubuntu","x":"Tested on: written from how the command works and standard systemd behaviour; not yet run on Debian or Ubuntu. Install the programs. This puts them in `/usr/local/bin` and asks for `sudo` once. To avoid `sudo`, add `-s -- --install-location ~/.local/bin` after `bash` and make sure `~/.local/bin` is on your `PATH`. Opti"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Arch Linux","u":"proxy-service.html#arch-linux","x":"Tested on: Arch Linux. The service files it writes pass `systemd-analyze verify`. The install and enable steps themselves have not been run on a real machine yet. Install the programs. Optional: shell sandbox. Install the service. Check it. Start at boot, not only at login. Arch has no firewall enabled by default. If y"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Fedora","u":"proxy-service.html#fedora","x":"Tested on: written from how the command works and standard systemd behaviour; not yet run on Fedora. Install the programs. Optional: shell sandbox. Install the service. Check it. Start at boot, not only at login. SELinux. A user service that runs a program from `/usr/local/bin` or `~/.local/bin` normally works with the"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"macOS","u":"proxy-service.html#macos","x":"Tested on: written from how the command works and standard launchd behaviour; not yet run on a Mac. Install the programs. The programs are not notarised by Apple. The installer downloads them with `curl`, which normally does not trigger Gatekeeper. If macOS still refuses to open one, remove the quarantine mark from it:"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Keep the phone gateway running too","u":"proxy-service.html#keep-the-phone-gateway-running-too","x":"The gateway is the address your phone connects to ( Control it from your phone ). To keep it up as well: This also switches remote control on and starts the gateway listening on your network (port 11541) with HTTPS: a real certificate when Tailscale is signed in, otherwise a self-signed one that works after a browser w"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Machines with no desktop","u":"proxy-service.html#machines-with-no-desktop","x":"Your sign-ins and keys go in the system keyring when there is one, and the configuration file holds only a reference to them. A server usually has no keyring: no Secret Service is running on your user session, or it cannot be unlocked without someone at the keyboard. Then the proxy falls back to a private file in the c"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Reach the setup page from another machine","u":"proxy-service.html#reach-the-setup-page-from-another-machine","x":"The setup page listens on `127.0.0.1` only and refuses to listen anywhere else, so do not try to expose it. To use it on a server, forward the port over SSH and open it in your own browser: Then browse to `http://127.0.0.1:11539` on your own machine. Over Tailscale the same forward works with the machine's Tailscale na"},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Update","u":"proxy-service.html#update","x":"Each program updates itself, then the service needs a restart to run the new copy: If `update` says permission denied for `/usr/local/bin`, run it with `sudo`, or install into `~/.local/bin` the first time. If you moved the programs, run `maple-harness service install` again so the file points at the new path."},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"proxy-service.html#troubleshooting","x":"`service install` says the systemd user manager is not reachable. There is no user session to attach to, typically over SSH or in a container. Log in through a normal session, or follow the SSH steps in the Debian and Ubuntu section. `maple-harness service print proxy` shows the file it would write so you can place it "},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Remove it","u":"proxy-service.html#remove-it","x":"This stops the services and removes the files it wrote. It leaves your configuration and sign-ins alone. On Linux, if you turned lingering on and no longer want it: `loginctl disable-linger \"$USER\"`."},{"p":"Keep it running in the background","h":"Advanced: one system-wide service","u":"proxy-service.html#advanced-one-system-wide-service","x":"Tested on: the unit text was checked with `systemd-analyze verify` on Arch Linux. Nothing else here has been run. Use the per-user service above unless you need this. A per-user service is the right default. If you want the proxy to run under its own account, for example on a server that nobody logs in to, you can writ"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Proxy settings","u":"proxy-settings.html","x":"This is the complete list of what you can change on the proxy: the cards on its setup page, the keys in `config.yaml`, the commands, and the response headers you can read. For a guided tour, read The proxy and its setup page first. The proxy and the harness share one `config.yaml`. A save on the setup page keeps the `h"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Settings at a glance","u":"proxy-settings.html#settings-at-a-glance","x":"Key Setup page card Default Helps `providers[].prompt_cache` Proxy settings, Caching `auto` Efficient `providers[].prompt_cache_key` Proxy settings, Caching off Efficient `providers[].echo_reasoning` Providers (config file) off Efficient, Effective `providers[].estimate_cost` Providers (config file) off Efficacious `pr"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Listeners and ports","u":"proxy-settings.html#listeners-and-ports","x":"Key Default What it is for `server.openai_compat` `127.0.0.1:11535` The OpenAI-compatible API (`/v1`). The harness, and any tool that speaks that API, use it `server.anthropic_compat` `127.0.0.1:11537` The Anthropic-compatible API. Claude Code and similar tools use it `server.webui` `127.0.0.1:11539` The setup page. It"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Providers and models","u":"proxy-settings.html#providers-and-models","x":"A provider is a backend that serves models. The setup page's Providers card builds these for you; this is what the keys mean if you edit the file. Key Default What it does `providers[].id` required The provider's name, for example `github-copilot` or `ollama-main` `providers[].type` required What it is: `github-copilot"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Caching","u":"proxy-settings.html#caching","x":"Long conversations resend the same beginning on every turn. Providers that cache charge far less for the part they have already seen. These settings help that happen; the Cache card shows whether it did. Key Setup page Default What it does Trade-off Helps `providers[].prompt_cache` Caching, a switch per provider `auto`"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Images and browser snapshots","u":"proxy-settings.html#images-and-browser-snapshots","x":"A model is charged for an image by its size in pixels, not by its file size, and a large image is scaled down by the provider before the model looks at it. Browser tools also return page snapshots (a text outline of the page) that are often the largest thing an agent resends. These settings shrink both. All of them are"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Cost visibility","u":"proxy-settings.html#cost-visibility","x":"Copilot states what a call cost, in AI units, on every response. Anthropic's own API never does — it reports token counts only. `/cost` (see What /cost tells you ) still shows a per-request figure for Anthropic when this is on, but it is a guess from Anthropic's published prices, not a bill, and is always shown labelle"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Fallbacks","u":"proxy-settings.html#fallbacks","x":"Key Setup page Default What it does Trade-off Helps `model_fallbacks` Fallbacks none For a model or alias, the names to try in order when it cannot answer: its account is out of chats or credit, the backend is overloaded, or it is unreachable A fallback may be a weaker or a different model, so results can differ. It ha"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Guards","u":"proxy-settings.html#guards","x":"Key Setup page Default What it does Trade-off Helps `context_guard` Guards, a three-way choice `off` For tools that do not know a model's window: `off` sends the request as it is, `truncate` drops the oldest whole turns until it fits, `error` refuses it with a plain message `truncate` silently forgets old turns; `error"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Budgets","u":"proxy-settings.html#budgets","x":"Key Setup page Default What it does Trade-off Helps `budgets[].provider` Budgets none Which provider the limit applies to Efficient `budgets[].max_requests_per_day` Budgets none (no limit) The most requests a day for that provider (days are UTC) When reached, requests are refused, or handed to that provider's fallback "},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Limits and hosts","u":"proxy-settings.html#limits-and-hosts","x":"These are read when the proxy starts. After changing them, stop and start `maple-proxy`. Key Setup page Default What it does Helps `server.rate_limit.enabled` Limits and hosts unset: off for this machine, on for other addresses Force the limit on or off Efficacious `server.rate_limit.requests_per_minute` Limits and hos"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"The usage log","u":"proxy-settings.html#the-usage-log","x":"Key Setup page Default What it does Helps `server.usage_ledger` Usage data, Location (or Limits and hosts, Usage log) on: `usage/maple_harness_proxy_usage_ledger.jsonl` in your Maple Harness config folder Where the usage log goes: a file that receives one line of numbers per request (model, tokens, cache figures, AI un"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Search by meaning","u":"proxy-settings.html#search-by-meaning","x":"Which model ranks the harness's searches by meaning. See Search by meaning for what it does, both models, their licences (EmbeddingGemma is under the Gemma Terms of Use and the Gemma Prohibited Use Policy , and its download needs a Hugging Face token from an account that accepted them) and hardware guidance: EmbeddingG"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"The setup page, card by card","u":"proxy-settings.html#the-setup-page-card-by-card","x":"Card What it shows and does Account Sign in to GitHub Copilot (a browser flow) or save an Anthropic API key. The Copilot plan and, where the plan has one, the chats left and when they reset Providers The configured backends and their models. Add a server, press Detect models , edit or delete Remote access The master sw"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Commands","u":"proxy-settings.html#commands","x":"`maple-proxy` with no command starts the server. The others: Command What it does `maple-proxy serve` Start the proxy (the default). Flags: `-config` a config file; `-logs` where logs go (`false`, `true`, `stdout`, or a file path); `-log-bodies` also log prompt and answer text, which is off by default because a prompt "},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Response headers","u":"proxy-settings.html#response-headers","x":"The proxy adds these to responses so you can see what happened to a request. Header Meaning `X-Maple-Served-By` The provider and model that answered, for example `github-copilot/gpt-6-sol` `X-Maple-Fallback-From` Present when a fallback answered: the model that was passed over, and why `X-Maple-Cache` `hit`, `partial`,"},{"p":"Proxy settings","h":"Files and environment","u":"proxy-settings.html#files-and-environment","x":"Where What `version` The configuration file's format version, currently `1` `~/.config/maple-harness/config.yaml` The proxy's and the harness's settings `~/.config/maple-harness/embeddings/` The installed built-in model for search by meaning, one folder per model, and the cache of computed vectors in `cache/`, private "},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"The proxy and its setup page","u":"proxy.html","x":"`maple-proxy` is the program that holds your sign-ins and talks to the models. The agent uses it, and so can any other tool that speaks the OpenAI or Anthropic API. Start it with: It stays in the foreground. Add `--logs=true` to watch requests, `--logs=/path/to.log` to keep a record, or `--logs=stdout` when something e"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"What it listens on","u":"proxy.html#what-it-listens-on","x":"Address What it is Who may use it `127.0.0.1:11539` The setup page This machine only. It refuses to listen anywhere else `127.0.0.1:11535` OpenAI-compatible API (`/v1`) This machine; other machines only with an access token `127.0.0.1:11537` Anthropic-compatible API This machine; other machines only with an access toke"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"The setup page","u":"proxy.html#the-setup-page","x":"Open `http://127.0.0.1:11539` on the machine running the proxy. A row of links at the top jumps to each part of the page, which comes in three groups: At a glance : the Savings, Cache and Sessions cards, and Usage data: what the proxy did for you. Set up : your account, your model servers (Providers), the Proxy setting"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Protect the setup page with a password","u":"proxy.html#protect-the-setup-page-with-a-password","x":"By default the setup page has no password: any program or browser on this computer can open it and change what it manages. To require one, open More > Protect this page , type a password twice (at least 8 characters) and press Turn on . The browser you did it in stays signed in for 30 days; any other browser shows a si"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Settings you may want","u":"proxy.html#settings-you-may-want","x":"Everything below is a card under Proxy settings on the setup page. Each control has a one-line explanation and a link back here. You do not need to edit any file. When you change something, nothing is written yet. Press Review changes to see exactly what will change in `config.yaml`, then Save settings , or Revert to t"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Prompt caching","u":"proxy.html#prompt-caching","x":"Long conversations resend the same beginning on every turn, and providers that cache charge much less for the part they have already seen. The Caching card has a switch per provider, shown only where it can be controlled (Claude through the Anthropic API or through Copilot); it is on by default. Keys: `prompt_cache`, `"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Model aliases","u":"proxy.html#model-aliases","x":"An alias is a short, fixed name for a model, so a tool can ask for `local` or `cloud` and you can change what that means later without touching the tool. Set them on the Model aliases card, or with `model_aliases` and a model's own `alias` in the file. See Providers and models ."},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Fallbacks","u":"proxy.html#fallbacks","x":"A fallback tells the proxy what to try when a model cannot answer: its account is out of chats or credit, the backend is overloaded, or it is unreachable. `model_fallbacks` lists other models to try for a model; `provider_fallbacks` sends one account's requests to another account, for example a second Copilot account, "},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Auto mode at the proxy","u":"proxy.html#auto-mode-at-the-proxy","x":"`model_aliases` and `model_fallbacks` together give you a name that is not a real model: request it and the proxy decides what answers, and every client picks up a change without being reconfigured. Maple Harness's `/model auto` picks a model itself by default (`harness.auto_model_resolution: client`). Set it to `proxy"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Guards","u":"proxy.html#guards","x":"The Context guard (`context_guard`) is for tools that do not know a model's window: it sends the request as is, drops the oldest turns until it fits, or refuses it. The Repeat guard (`degenerate_guard`, off by default) stops a reply that has collapsed into repeating itself. See Guards ."},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Keeping the cache useful","u":"proxy.html#keeping-the-cache-useful","x":"Two settings on the Guards card help a provider's prompt cache on long conversations: the Prefix stabiliser (`prefix_stabilizer`, on by default) keeps the start of each request the same from turn to turn, and Trim old tool results (`context_trim`, off by default) replaces old, large tool results with a short note. Resp"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Images and browser snapshots","u":"proxy.html#images-and-browser-snapshots","x":"Four choices on the Guards card, all off by default, shrink images and browser page snapshots before they are sent: `image_resize_tool`, `image_resize_user`, `image_superseded` and `snapshot_trim`. Smaller images cost fewer tokens, and small text in a screenshot may become harder to read. See Images and browser snapsho"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Seeing how the cache is doing","u":"proxy.html#seeing-how-the-cache-is-doing","x":"The Cache card shows today's hit rate, the tokens read from and written to the cache, a table per model and per client, and the latest times the cache was lost, with the reason. `maple-proxy cache` prints the same in a terminal, and responses that are not streamed carry `X-Maple-Cache`. Only counts and labels are kept,"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Seeing cost per session","u":"proxy.html#seeing-cost-per-session","x":"The Sessions card groups AI units, tokens and requests by session. Maple Harness sends a random session id with every request (`X-Maple-Session`), and a subagent sends its parent's id, so its spend counts toward the session that started it. Any other client can send the header too. See What /cost tells you ."},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Seeing what it saves","u":"proxy.html#seeing-what-it-saves","x":"The Savings card, first on the page, shows what the proxy's techniques saved and what they cost, across every session on this proxy. Every figure is tagged measured (a count, or what the provider billed) or estimated (a value in AI units, estimated from what your own requests cost). The estimate is never more than what"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Daily budgets","u":"proxy.html#daily-budgets","x":"For each provider you can cap requests per day, AI units per day, or both (days are UTC). At the limit, requests are refused or handed to its fallback. Key: `budgets`. See Budgets ."},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Limits and hosts (restart required)","u":"proxy.html#limits-and-hosts-restart-required","x":"The rate limit (off for this machine, on for other machines by default) and allowed hosts (extra names the proxy answers to) are read when the proxy starts, so restart it after saving. The usage log , one line of counts per request, is on by default and applies as soon as you save. See Limits and hosts , The usage log "},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Using it from another machine","u":"proxy.html#using-it-from-another-machine","x":"By default only this machine can reach the model APIs. To let another machine on your network use them, set the listen address and create an access token with the `proxy` scope on the setup page: The proxy refuses to start on a non-loopback address until such a token exists, and callers send it as `Authorization: Beare"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Check the cache yourself","u":"proxy.html#check-the-cache-yourself","x":"Providers cache the start of a request that they have seen recently, and reading from that cache costs a fraction of processing it again. Whether that is working for your account and your model is easy to see: It sends the same small request twice, a few seconds apart, through the running proxy, and prints what each ca"},{"p":"The proxy and its setup page","h":"Other useful commands","u":"proxy.html#other-useful-commands","x":""},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Control it from your phone","u":"remote-control.html","x":"Share a session and drive it from your phone's browser or from another machine. You can approve tool calls (with a look at exactly what will change), open several sessions in one list, and start new ones in folders you have allowed."},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Recommended setup: Tailscale","u":"remote-control.html#recommended-setup-tailscale","x":"Put the computer and the phone on the same Tailscale network. Nothing is exposed to your local network or the internet, the traffic is already encrypted, and Tailscale can issue a real HTTPS certificate for your machine, which is what lets a phone show notifications with the page closed. Install Tailscale on the comput"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Sharing a session","u":"remote-control.html#sharing-a-session","x":"Inside a running session, type: `/remote` starts the gateway if it is not running. The gateway is the one address on your machine that lists every shared session, so a phone sees them all. A session shared through it stays shared until you turn it off or close the session. To start without any session open, use `maple-"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Pairing","u":"remote-control.html#pairing","x":"A pairing code (eight characters like `K7QM-4XPT`) works once and expires after five minutes. The phone trades it for a token of its own, which you can see and revoke in the device list on the setup page at `http://127.0.0.1:11539`. Scan the QR code, open the link, or type the code. Turning Remote control on in the set"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Sharing one session","u":"remote-control.html#sharing-one-session","x":"You can give someone a link to one session, and nothing else. On the remote page, open the session and tap Share (the arrow button at the top; a watch-only device does not have it). Give the share a label, such as who it is for, and choose what they can do: Watch only (the default): they follow the session live and can"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Starting sessions from the phone","u":"remote-control.html#starting-sessions-from-the-phone","x":"The New session sheet starts a session in a folder you have allowed: A phone can only start sessions in those folders, and never anywhere else. A folder inside one of them that does not exist yet, such as a new project, is created when the session starts. Resuming a saved session whose folder has been deleted is refuse"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Starting in yolo mode","u":"remote-control.html#starting-in-yolo-mode","x":"Yolo mode allows every tool call that would otherwise ask, without asking. Your `deny` rules still apply. Because a stray tap on a phone is easy, starting a session as yolo from the phone is off until you turn it on for the machine: Restart the gateway after changing it. The sheet then offers Start in yolo mode , asks "},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Notifications","u":"remote-control.html#notifications","x":"Turn on Notify me when this session needs me in the session sheet. When the page is in the background you get a notification, a vibration and a count in the tab title when a session needs approval, stops with an error or finishes. It says only that, plus the project's name, never your commands or paths. With the page c"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Using the page","u":"remote-control.html#using-the-page","x":"Replies render as Markdown as they stream: headings, lists, tables and code blocks. Code blocks and tables have Copy buttons, and each reply has Copy Markdown . Send with in the session sheet chooses the key that sends: Ctrl/Cmd+Enter (the default), Enter, Shift+Enter or a combination you record. The Send button always"},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"Attach from another computer","u":"remote-control.html#attach-from-another-computer","x":""},{"p":"Control it from your phone","h":"If it does not work","u":"remote-control.html#if-it-does-not-work","x":"The phone cannot reach the address. Check that both devices are on the same Tailscale network, or the same local network for the LAN address, and that a firewall is not blocking port 11541. Certificate warning on the Tailscale name. HTTPS certificates are not enabled for your tailnet yet; see step 2. `+ New session` is"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"Drive it from another program","u":"rpc.html","x":"`maple-harness --rpc` reads commands from another process instead of a person, and writes back everything that process needs to follow along: the model's own output, tool calls and results, questions that need an answer, and the running cost — as it happens, not only once the whole session ends. It is how a CI step, an"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"Start it","u":"rpc.html#start-it","x":"It never starts on its own; it is meant to be the child of whatever is driving it. One JSON object per line comes in on stdin, one JSON object per line goes out on stdout. Nothing else is written to stdout — diagnostics and notes go to stderr, the same convention `--prompt` uses, so a plain `harness --rpc < commands.js"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"Commands","u":"rpc.html#commands","x":"Every line you send is one JSON object with a `cmd` field, and an optional `id` you choose — it comes back on the response so you can match one to the other. `cmd` What it does `prompt` Starts a new turn with `text`. Rejected while one is already running — say `steer` or `follow_up` instead, because they mean different"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"Events","u":"rpc.html#events","x":"What the turn itself does streams as further lines, `\"type\": \"event\"`, tagged by `event`: `event` Carries `assistant_delta` / `assistant_done` `text` (and `thinking`, when the model reasoned) `tool_call` / `tool_result` `tool`, `tool_arg` `steered` `text` — a `steer` command landed and is now part of the conversation `"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"A permission question","u":"rpc.html#a-permission-question","x":"A tool call that needs approval under your permission settings — the same settings `--yolo` and the interface's own mode control (ask/auto/yolo) govern — is not decided for you. It arrives as an event: Answer it the same way a person would: and a `permission_resolved` event confirms it, with `allow` and `always`. There"},{"p":"Drive it from another program","h":"What it is not","u":"rpc.html#what-it-is-not","x":"This is a local pipe between a parent process and the child it started — there is no listening address and no token, because whoever can write to this process's stdin already has everything a shell here would give them. If you want a session another device can join over the network instead, see Control it from your pho"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"The shell sandbox","u":"sandbox.html","x":"The agent runs shell commands for you through its `bash` tool. Those commands run as you, with your files, your logins and your network. The sandbox contains them: when it is on, a command the model runs can change your project but not the rest of your computer, and cannot read the places where your credentials live. I"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"What it does","u":"sandbox.html#what-it-does","x":"While the sandbox is on, every `bash` command runs with these limits: What contained commands get Writable Your project folder, a private temporary folder, and the usual build caches under your home folder (`~/.cache`, `~/go`, `~/.npm`, `~/.cargo`, `~/.gradle`, `~/.m2` and similar), so builds and installs into the proj"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"What it breaks","u":"sandbox.html#what-it-breaks","x":"Anything that needs a hidden login, or writes outside the project, fails while the sandbox is on: Tool Why it fails What to do `git push`, `git pull` over SSH, `ssh`, `scp` `~/.ssh` is hidden, so there is no key and no known hosts Push yourself, allow `~/.ssh` for the session, or run that one command outside the sandbo"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"Turning it on","u":"sandbox.html#turning-it-on","x":"Setting What it does `/sandbox auto` Contain commands in this session. Without a backend (see Platforms ) commands still run, uncontained, and the first result says so `/sandbox strict` Contain commands in this session, and refuse to run any command when there is no backend `/sandbox off` No containment in this session"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"Contain commands whenever nobody approves them","u":"sandbox.html#contain-commands-whenever-nobody-approves-them","x":"In `ask` mode you see each command before it runs. In `auto` and `yolo` mode commands run without a person approving each one, and that is where containment matters most. This option turns the sandbox on exactly then: With it on, and `sandbox.mode` not set: In `auto` or `yolo` mode, commands are contained (as with `aut"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"When a command is blocked","u":"sandbox.html#when-a-command-is-blocked","x":"When a contained command fails because of the sandbox, the result says so in plain words, for you and for the model. For example: A `git push` over SSH blocked by the sandbox: the note says why, and the model stops and tells you what it needed. It says \"blocked\" only when the failure names a place the sandbox hides or "},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"Recommended setup","u":"sandbox.html#recommended-setup","x":"Install the backend (below) and run `/sandbox` or `maple-harness doctor` to check it works. If you use `auto` mode, turn on `sandbox.when_unattended`. Set a password for the proxy's setup page ( More > Protect this page , or `maple-proxy setup-password set`). The sandbox keeps contained commands away from the page; the"},{"p":"The shell sandbox","h":"Platforms","u":"sandbox.html#platforms","x":"System Backend Status Linux bubblewrap (optional install, below) Verified. Blocking the setup page needs Linux 6.7 or later; on an older kernel commands can still reach it, and `/sandbox` says so macOS The built-in `sandbox-exec`, nothing to install Built, but not yet run on a Mac by us: treat it as unverified Windows "},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"See and reduce what a task costs","u":"saving-tokens.html","x":"Long agent tasks spend most of their money re-reading a growing conversation on every turn. This page shows you where your spend goes, what the numbers mean, and a few habits that keep it down. It makes no promise about how much you will save: that depends on your tasks and your models, so measure your own."},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"See where your spend goes","u":"saving-tokens.html#see-where-your-spend-goes","x":"In the agent: `/cost` shows the session's totals, the most recent requests with their cache hit rate, any retries, and roughly where the input went (instructions, tool descriptions, your messages, tool output). `/context` shows the window and what has been spent so far, including helper agents. In the proxy: the Cache "},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"What the numbers mean","u":"saving-tokens.html#what-the-numbers-mean","x":"Hit rate. The share of a request's input that the provider served from its cache instead of processing again. A long task with a stable start should sit high; it drops when something early in the request changes. Tokens written and read. The first time the provider sees a stretch of your conversation it stores it, whic"},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"Habits that keep costs down","u":"saving-tokens.html#habits-that-keep-costs-down","x":"Keep one model for a long task. Switching models mid-task starts the provider's cache over, and the whole conversation is written again. `/model auto` keeps your model when it fits the job for this reason. Start a new task in a new session. A fresh session starts with a small conversation. Carrying an old, finished tas"},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"What we do not do","u":"saving-tokens.html#what-we-do-not-do","x":"Maple Harness does not put a filter between your commands and the model, and does not ask the model to talk in shorthand. Very large outputs are cut at a limit, with a note saying so."},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"What others have measured","u":"saving-tokens.html#what-others-have-measured","x":"These are other people's measurements, of other tools, on other models and workloads. They are here for context, not as a claim about Maple. rtk , a filter for shell command output that advertises 60 to 90% fewer tokens. In JetBrains' benchmark (Denis Shiryaev, July 2026; 86 tasks, 425 billed trials, Claude Code with `"},{"p":"See and reduce what a task costs","h":"Related","u":"saving-tokens.html#related","x":"What /cost tells you · Settings by goal · Your usage data · Check it yourself · Measure with bench · Proxy settings · Harness settings · The proxy and its setup page · Choose a model · Subagents · Troubleshooting"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Search by meaning","u":"search-by-meaning.html","x":"The agent looks things up with three tools: `history_search` (older parts of the conversation that were moved out of view to make room), `recall` (memory notes) and `graph_search` (the project index of files and symbols). On their own they match words, so a search for \"the login bug\" misses a message about \"authenticat"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"The two models","u":"search-by-meaning.html#the-two-models","x":"bge-small-en-v1.5 (default) EmbeddingGemma 300M (optional) Runs in the proxy itself, on the CPU; nothing else to install the proxy itself, on the CPU, or your own embeddings server (Ollama, LM Studio and others) Download about 128 MB, once; about 33 MB is kept on disk about 1.2 GB, once; about 304 MB is kept on disk. N"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Choose the engine","u":"search-by-meaning.html#choose-the-engine","x":"The Search by meaning card has two engines: Built-in (Go): the proxy runs the model itself, on the CPU, with nothing else to install. Pick bge-small or EmbeddingGemma, download it, and press Use this model . Your own server (OpenAI-compatible): the proxy sends the text to an embeddings server you already run on this ma"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Set it up","u":"search-by-meaning.html#set-it-up","x":"From the setup page: open the proxy's setup page, find the Search by meaning card, keep Built-in (Go) selected and press Download on bge-small-en-v1.5. The card shows the download's progress (with Cancel ), this machine's memory and CPU cores, and a note if the machine is below what the model is comfortable on. It is i"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Use EmbeddingGemma built in","u":"search-by-meaning.html#use-embeddinggemma-built-in","x":"EmbeddingGemma is gated on Hugging Face: Google asks everyone to accept its terms before downloading it. Sign in to Hugging Face , open the EmbeddingGemma 300M page and accept the terms there. Read the Gemma Terms of Use and the Gemma Prohibited Use Policy first. Create a token with read access at huggingface.co/settin"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Use your own server","u":"search-by-meaning.html#use-your-own-server","x":"Any server with an OpenAI-compatible `/v1/embeddings` works, for example: Server Base URL (default port) Model name, for example Ollama `http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1` `embeddinggemma` (after `ollama pull embeddinggemma`) LM Studio `http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1` the embedding model you loaded llama.cpp's `llama-server` (starte"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"When something goes wrong","u":"search-by-meaning.html#when-something-goes-wrong","x":"The card's status line, its messages and the terminal commands use the same words. Each problem says what to do: What you see What to do The network is not reachable Check the connection (and any VPN), then try again The secure connection could not be verified Check the machine's clock and any network filter that inter"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Is my machine enough?","u":"search-by-meaning.html#is-my-machine-enough","x":"The proxy reads the machine's total and free memory, its CPU cores, its processor type and operating system, and shows them on the card. Before a download it warns, and never blocks, when the machine is below these: Model Comfortable from Basis bge-small-en-v1.5 4 GB of memory and 2 CPU cores measured: about 150 MB pea"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"What changes for the agent","u":"search-by-meaning.html#what-changes-for-the-agent","x":"Tool With a model Without one `history_search` Results ranked by words and meaning combined. The filters (role, tool, path, turn range) apply first, as before Words only, unchanged `recall` Gains a `query` argument that returns the eight closest notes by words and meaning. Without `query` it lists notes as before No `q"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Measured results","u":"search-by-meaning.html#measured-results","x":"A retrieval test, measured on 28 September 2026: queries worded differently from what they look for, over a 43-message coding conversation (24 queries), 24 project memory notes (16 queries), and this project's own index of about 7,100 files and symbols (16 queries). Each query was run twice through the real tools, word"},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"The proxy's cache","u":"search-by-meaning.html#the-proxys-cache","x":"The proxy keeps every vector it computes, so a text it has already seen is answered at once instead of computed again: the harness re-reading the same messages, notes or files costs almost nothing the second time. In a request with several texts, only the new ones are computed, and the answer keeps the order you sent. "},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Where things are kept, and removing them","u":"search-by-meaning.html#where-things-are-kept-and-removing-them","x":"What Where Removed by A built-in model `embeddings/bge-small-en-v1.5/` or `embeddings/embeddinggemma-300m/` in your Maple Harness config folder (folder private to you, files readable only by you) Remove on the card, or `maple-proxy embeddings remove <name>` The Hugging Face token, and your server's API key the proxy's "},{"p":"Search by meaning","h":"Privacy","u":"search-by-meaning.html#privacy","x":"Text goes from the harness to the proxy it already uses, and no further than a model on this machine or your own network. The built-in models run inside the proxy. With your own server, the proxy forwards only to an address it can tell is local, and refuses one that is not. When it forwards, the proxy does not follow a"},{"p":"Security","h":"Security","u":"security.html","x":"An agent runs commands and writes files on your machine; that is its job. This page says what Maple Harness does to keep that safe, and what you should do."},{"p":"Security","h":"Reporting a problem","u":"security.html#reporting-a-problem","x":"Email Kinn Coelho Juliao at <kinncj@gmail.com> with what you found, how to reproduce it, and the version (`maple-harness version`). Please allow a reasonable time to respond before disclosing it publicly. Reports are handled in confidence."},{"p":"Security","h":"What it does for you","u":"security.html#what-it-does-for-you","x":"It asks first. Every tool call passes your rules. By default it asks before writing a file or running a command, and a `deny` rule is absolute. `--yolo` skips the asking, never the denying, and it must be given on the command line: a configuration file can never turn it on by itself. The shell can be contained. With bu"},{"p":"Security","h":"What you should do","u":"security.html#what-you-should-do","x":"Treat a device token like an SSH key. It is a shell on your computer. Prefer pairing codes, keep tokens out of chats and screenshots, and revoke a lost phone straight away. Use Tailscale, or another private network, for remote control. Do not forward the port through your router to the internet. See Control it from you"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"Sign in","u":"sign-in.html","x":"You can sign in from the terminal or from the proxy's setup page at `http://127.0.0.1:11539`. Either way, the proxy holds the credential and the agent only asks it for models. Signing in again refreshes the same account — the models it can use, its plan, its allowance — rather than adding another."},{"p":"Sign in","h":"GitHub Copilot","u":"sign-in.html#github-copilot","x":"The command prints a code and a link. Open the link, enter the code, and approve. The agent then asks Copilot which models your plan allows and configures exactly those. Free plan. It works, including Copilot's auto mode. The setup page shows how many chats you have left and when they reset, and the status bar in the a"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"Claude (Anthropic API key)","u":"sign-in.html#claude-anthropic-api-key","x":"It asks for an API key (typed hidden), checks it with Anthropic, and configures the models that key can call. You can also paste it into the Anthropic (Claude) card on the setup page. A claude.ai or Claude Code subscription is not an API key and cannot be used here. Create a key in the Anthropic Console. An account wit"},{"p":"Sign in","h":"More than one account","u":"sign-in.html#more-than-one-account","x":"Add a second (or third) account of the same provider — another GitHub Copilot seat, another Anthropic API key — and give it a name, so you can tell it apart from the others. A name can be used by one account only, across all providers, and cannot be a provider's id: On the setup page, each provider's card shows \"+ Add "},{"p":"Sign in","h":"Where the credentials are kept","u":"sign-in.html#where-the-credentials-are-kept","x":"Keys and sign-ins go in your operating system's keyring (Keychain on macOS, the Secret Service on Linux). The configuration file holds only a reference such as `${secret:anthropic-key}`, never the key, so it is safe to paste into an issue. Without a keyring they fall back to a private file, and the proxy tells you. `ma"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Subagents","u":"subagents.html","x":"A subagent is a second session that the main one hands a small, self-contained job to: \"find where the retry logic lives\", \"review this diff\", \"plan the change\". It has its own instructions, its own list of tools and optionally its own model. It does not see your conversation, and only its final report comes back, so a"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"The ones you already have","u":"subagents.html#the-ones-you-already-have","x":"Name What it does `general-purpose` Takes any self-contained task. Has the same tools as the main session `explore` Reads and searches the project and answers questions about it. Cannot change anything `plan` Studies the code and returns a step-by-step plan. Cannot change anything The model uses them by itself when a j"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Watching them work","u":"subagents.html#watching-them-work","x":"While a subagent runs, a small list appears just above the input box (which does not move): `◯` is running, `!` is waiting for you, `✓` finished, `✗` failed or stopped. Press the down arrow from an empty input box (or `ctrl+t` from anywhere) to move into the list, then: the arrow keys choose between `main` and a subage"},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Make your own","u":"subagents.html#make-your-own","x":"Type `/agents`. You see every agent with where it lives, and can view, create, edit or delete them. Creating one asks where it should live (this project, or all your projects), a name, a description (this is what tells the model when to use it), which tools it may use (all, read-only, or a list you tick), which model, "},{"p":"Subagents","h":"Settings","u":"subagents.html#settings","x":"Setting Default Meaning `harness.subagents.max_parallel` `3` How many run at the same time when the model hands out several jobs at once `harness.subagents.max_turns` `50` The most turns any one subagent may take When the model gives out several jobs in one message they run together, and their reports come back in the "},{"p":"Settings by goal","h":"Settings by goal","u":"three-es.html","x":"Maple Harness aims at three things at once: to be efficient (fewer tokens for the same work), efficacious (the work stays as good, and defaults are safe) and effective (whole tasks succeed and waste stops early). This page maps a goal to the settings and commands that serve it. It makes no promise about your bill: resu"},{"p":"Settings by goal","h":"I want to spend less on long runs","u":"three-es.html#i-want-to-spend-less-on-long-runs","x":"Do this Where Trade-off Keep one model for a long task instead of switching `/model <name>`, or `/model auto` (it keeps your model when it fits) A fixed model may be more than a small job needs See where the money goes `/cost`, the setup page's Cache card, `maple-proxy cache` None Keep the provider's cache working `pro"},{"p":"Settings by goal","h":"I want the work to stay as good","u":"three-es.html#i-want-the-work-to-stay-as-good","x":"Do this Where Trade-off Let the compiler check the model's edits `lsp` (on by default), `/lsp` to see what is running The first edit starts the server in the background Keep the safe defaults `permission.mode: ask`, `sandbox.mode`, `file_policy.*` More questions, fewer surprises Prefer the tools that show a diff `bash_"},{"p":"Settings by goal","h":"I want to stop runaways","u":"three-es.html#i-want-to-stop-runaways","x":"Do this Where Trade-off End a turn that keeps failing the same way, with a summary `loop_guard.mode: stop`, `loop_guard.stop_after` See above Cut a reply that repeats itself `degenerate_guard` (the repeat guard) on the proxy's Guards card A repetitive answer could be cut short Refuse or trim a request bigger than the m"},{"p":"Settings by goal","h":"I want results I can rely on","u":"three-es.html#i-want-results-i-can-rely-on","x":"Do this Where Trade-off Get told when a session needs you `notifications.*`, `maple-harness notify test` None Resume where you left off `--continue`, `--resume`, `/sessions` Old sessions take disk; `cleanup` removes them Keep a checklist for a big request `ledger: auto`, `/ledger` One extra model call Make the model ca"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html","x":"Start with the checker. It looks at your configuration, the proxy, sign-ins, ports, remote control and the sandbox, and says what to do about each problem it finds: Two commands inside a session answer most of the rest: `maple-proxy serve --logs=true` shows every request and the reason behind every failed one."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"The proxy","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-proxy","x":"The agent says the proxy is unreachable. Nothing answers at the configured address. `/proxy` shows where the agent is looking, and `/proxy start` starts one. This usually means `server.openai_compat` in `config.yaml` differs from where a proxy was started. The setup page at `127.0.0.1:11539` does not load. Check the pr"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Signing in","u":"troubleshooting.html#signing-in","x":"Copilot sign-in never finishes. Open the link it printed and enter the code within ten minutes, then run `maple-harness auth login github-copilot` again. A `403` means the account has no Copilot seat, or the grant was revoked; signing in again is the first thing to try. \"This account has no chat models enabled for Copi"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Models","u":"troubleshooting.html#models","x":"The context window shows 0, or history is trimmed too early. A window of 0 means unknown. Leave `context_window` out of `config.yaml` so the proxy asks the server, and check `/context`. If the server reports nothing, set it by hand. A local model repeats itself or stops without finishing. Small models do this. Use them"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Cost","u":"troubleshooting.html#cost","x":""},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"A run cost more than I expected","u":"troubleshooting.html#a-run-cost-more-than-i-expected","x":"Type `/cost`. It shows what the session has spent, the last few requests with how much of each was served from the provider's cache, and where the input goes. A request marked \"low cache hit\" or \"model changed\" is where the cache was lost: a new model starts with a cold cache. To keep it, use `/model fixed` (or pick a "},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"The agent keeps retrying the same failing command","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-agent-keeps-retrying-the-same-failing-command","x":"When the same problem fails again and again, Maple Harness steps in. After three similar failures it adds a note to the result telling the model to stop trying variations, say what it is assuming, check that with a cheap read-only command, and choose a different approach. After six, it ends the turn with a summary of w"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Installing and updating","u":"troubleshooting.html#installing-and-updating","x":"`checksum mismatch`. The installer stops and leaves the existing files untouched. A truncated download is the usual cause; run it again. `permission denied` writing to `/usr/local/bin`. Use `--install-location ~/.local/bin`, or run the installer with `sudo`. macOS says a downloaded binary cannot be opened. Binaries dow"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Remote control","u":"troubleshooting.html#remote-control","x":"The phone cannot reach the address. Check both devices are on the same Tailscale network (or the same local network for a LAN address) and that a firewall is not blocking port 11541. Pairing shows a link that does not load. The gateway may have stopped. `/remote pair` starts it again; or run `maple-harness gateway star"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"The shell sandbox","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-shell-sandbox","x":"`/sandbox` says bubblewrap is missing, or installed but not working. It is optional; the install command for your system is in Install . \"Installed but does not work\" usually means unprivileged user namespaces are blocked, for example in a container. Until it works, commands run uncontained. A command fails inside a sh"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Disk space","u":"troubleshooting.html#disk-space","x":"The configuration directory (`~/.config/maple-harness`) holds saved sessions, memory notes and logs. To see how much it uses and clear old sessions, run `maple-harness cleanup --help`."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"More help on other pages","u":"troubleshooting.html#more-help-on-other-pages","x":"Some pages have their own troubleshooting section for the feature they cover: Language servers: Troubleshooting : a server is not found, or its errors do not show up. Search by meaning: When something goes wrong . The shell sandbox: When a command is blocked . Control it from your phone: If it does not work . Keep it r"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Your usage data","u":"usage-export.html","x":"The usage log is on by default: one JSON object per request, in your own files — not locked inside Maple. It holds counts and labels only (model, tokens, cache figures, AI units, timing), never prompts or answers. This page is about where it lives, how long it is kept, and actually using that data: querying it directly"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Where the files are","u":"usage-export.html#where-the-files-are","x":"By default the log is in the `usage` folder of your Maple Harness config folder (`~/.config/maple-harness/usage/` on Linux and macOS), one file per UTC day with a name nothing else uses: `maple_harness_proxy_usage_ledger-2026-09-28.jsonl`, `maple_harness_proxy_usage_ledger-2026-09-29.jsonl`, and so on. The folder is cr"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"How long it is kept","u":"usage-export.html#how-long-it-is-kept","x":"`server.usage_retention_days` ( Keep on the Usage data card) is how many days of daily files to keep. Older daily files are deleted when the proxy starts and then once a day, the proxy logs one line saying what it removed, and each cleanup that deletes files is recorded in the audit log (how many, and the folder). `0` "},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Turn it off","u":"usage-export.html#turn-it-off","x":"Press Turn off on the Usage data card (or on the one-time notice the setup page shows after upgrading), or set `usage_ledger: off` in `config.yaml`. For one run of the proxy, `CCH_USAGE_LEDGER=off` in its environment does the same (and `CCH_USAGE_LEDGER=/some/path` sends that run's log elsewhere). Nothing already writt"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"From the setup page","u":"usage-export.html#from-the-setup-page","x":"The proxy's setup page has a Usage data card with everything below without a terminal: whether the log is on and where; how much disk the daily files take, how many there are and the days they cover, with an estimate at the retention limit once there is a whole day of data; Location (Move, Use the default place, Turn o"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Query it directly with DuckDB","u":"usage-export.html#query-it-directly-with-duckdb","x":"This is the fastest way to ask a real question of your own data — no export step, no code. DuckDB reads JSON Lines natively. Start by creating a typed view named `usage`: `usage schema --format sql` prints a `CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW usage AS ...` statement over every daily file (it finds them the same way `usage export`"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Cost and savings","u":"usage-export.html#cost-and-savings","x":"Each line carries what the request cost and, as estimates, what the proxy's techniques saved and cost on it: the same figures the setup page's Savings card and `maple-proxy savings` show, per request. The card adds them up; the log keeps them one request at a time, so you can add them up your own way. A sum over a day "},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"The usage record schema","u":"usage-export.html#the-usage-record-schema","x":"Every line of the log, and every row of an export, is one usage record. The schema is versioned; this is version 3 . Later versions may add columns at the end, and never rename, remove, reorder or retype one. Version 2 added the columns from `schema_version` on: the session, the cache-warming flag, a list-price estimat"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Export to CSV or Parquet","u":"usage-export.html#export-to-csv-or-parquet","x":"For handing the data to a tool that cannot read JSON Lines directly: `--since`/`--until` are UTC-day boundaries (`YYYY-MM-DD`); `--until` is exclusive. Without `--path`, it finds the log the same way the running proxy does (`usage_ledger` from your `config.yaml`, the default place when that is unset, and `CCH_USAGE_LED"},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Keep it tidy: prune","u":"usage-export.html#keep-it-tidy-prune","x":"The proxy already deletes daily files older than `server.usage_retention_days` (see How long it is kept ). To remove more now, by hand: Only rotated, dated files are ever candidates for deletion. A plain, undated file left over from before rotation existed is never touched by `prune` — if you want that gone, remove it "},{"p":"Your usage data","h":"Wire it into Grafana","u":"usage-export.html#wire-it-into-grafana","x":"Point an existing Grafana plugin at your files rather than a bespoke Maple Harness dashboard, so it stays current with however you actually run Grafana: Infinity (maintained by Grafana Labs, in the official plugin catalog) reads CSV and JSON directly, by path or URL, and is the simpler route: Add it as a data source, p"}];
